       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. You've confirmed it, but 4000 mg per dose is 16,000 mg/day, and no indication supports that. It's in the range where ibuprofen toxicity is expected, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS depression. You also haven't given a clinical rationale, and a confirmation alone doesn't change the risk to the patient.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n\n- **Enter a standard dose.** I can place 400 mg PO QID (1,600 mg/day) or 800 mg PO QID (3,200 mg/day, the maximum) now.\n- **Discuss it with pharmacy.** If you think this unusual dose is intended, a pharmacist or the attending can review it and enter it through the hospital's high-dose override process.\n- **Tell me what you're treating.** If the goal is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect, I can help you work out a safer regimen, such as scheduled acetaminophen alongside ibuprofen or a different agent.\n\nIf this was a typo, give me the dose you meant and I'll enter it right away."
